On 07/05/2018 23:24, Juergen Fenn wrote:
> I think that's a good point. Given the pace of development in LaTeX
> nowadays, I wonder whether this is the way to build a new standard. Who
> cares about latexrelease, \UseRawInputEncoding, etc. after all?

Hopefully no-one cares much and few people notice.
But that is the point and why core systems need to be maintained and=20
occasionally updated.

While UTF-8 was first designed in 1992, it's fairly certain that no one=20
was writing latex documents in UTF-8 when latex2e was designed in 1993.

Now virtually all editors and operating systems default to UTF-8. The=20
fact that for most users, most of the time, "it just works", required=20
some internal changes and those changes may possibly affect documents=20
doing odd things in some cases. Hence the latexrelease and=20
UseRawInputEncoding possibilities for reverting some of those internal=20
changes, but they are not something most users should ever expect to use.

What this means for bibtex isn't so clear but the general issue of=20
whether you need to update the core system and how to manage=20
compatibility in edge cases that are adversely affected by any change=20
applies of course to any software not just bibtex of latex.


David

